  <!-- TAB 3: THE SCIENCE PANEL -->
  <section id="tab-panel-science" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-btn-science" class="tab-panel hidden space-y-6">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-2xl border border-hairline shadow-sm space-y-3">
        <div class="text-2xl">🔬</div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink">The 2023 ACS Nano Discovery: Billions of Particles</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
          In 2023, researchers at the University of Nebraska-Lincoln published groundbreaking findings in <em>ACS Nano</em> (Hussain et al.). Testing FDA-approved polypropylene (#5) containers, they discovered that just 3 minutes of microwave heating released up to <strong>4.22 million microplastics</strong> and <strong>2.11 billion nanoplastics per cm²</strong> into food simulant liquids.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
          Nanoplastics smaller than 1,000 nanometers easily cross the human gut barrier, enter hepatic circulation, and have been detected in blood vessels, placental tissue, and cardiac arterial plaques.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-2xl border border-hairline shadow-sm space-y-3">
        <div class="text-2xl">🔥</div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink">The Fat Extraction Multiplier: Why Oils Melt Plastic</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
          While water is thermodynamically limited to 100°C (212°F) at standard atmospheric pressure, lipids, gravies, and oils have no such barrier. Under microwave radiation, culinary oils superheat past <strong>180°C to 200°C (356°F–392°F)</strong> in seconds.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
          Because polypropylene softens at 120°C and melts at 160°C, molten fat burns directly into the plastic surface, creating the familiar permanent cloudy white haze and pitting. Lipids also act as organic non-polar solvents, drawing out lipophilic phthalate and antioxidant plasticizers up to <strong>20× faster</strong> than water.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-2xl border border-hairline shadow-sm space-y-3">
        <div class="text-2xl">🫧</div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink">Dishwasher Detergent Etching (pH 10–11)</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
          Residential dishwashers use highly alkaline caustic detergents (pH 10–11) designed to saponify grease. Combined with 65°C heated water and 80°C forced-air drying cycles, repeated washing strips the plastic's protective injection-molded outer skin layer.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
          This causes microscopic surface craze-cracking. Once a plastic container becomes cloudy or brittle from the dishwasher, subsequent microwave cycles shed exponentially more plastic shards directly into warm meals.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-2xl border border-hairline shadow-sm space-y-3">
        <div class="text-2xl">🏷️</div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink">The 'Microwave-Safe' Legal Myth</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
          Consumers frequently assume the words 'Microwave-Safe' mean zero chemicals or particles enter food. Under US FDA 21 CFR § 177 and European EFSA regulations, the stamp only certifies that <em>the container itself will not melt, warp, or catch fire</em> under laboratory baseline conditions.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
          The certification does not test for nanoplastic shedding, endocrine disruptor release in scratched containers, or fat-catalyzed migration.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

